<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Neural Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Neural Plasticity</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">2090-5904</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1687-5443</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">11530882</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565387</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.2001.111</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>The Motor System: The Whole and its Parts</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Otten</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"/><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1">Department of Medical Physiology, University of Groningen, , The Netherlands</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*E. Otten: <email>e.otten@med.rug.nl</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2001</year></pub-date><volume>8</volume><issue>1-2</issue><fpage>111</fpage><lpage>119</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2001.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2001</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>Our knowledge of components of the humanmotor system has been growing steadily, but ourunderstanding of its integration into a system islagging behind. It is suggested that a combinationof measurements of forces and movements of themotor system in a functionally meaningfulenvironment in conjunction with computersimulations of the motor system may help us inunderstanding motor system properties. Neurotraumacan be seen as a natural deviation, withrecovery as a slow path to yet another deviantstate of the motor system. In that form they maybe useful in explaining the close interactionbetween form and function of the human motorsystem.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>